## The curfew fires once, on Chapter 3's arrival (`before keep`): every
## later daytime entry to the hallway — the raid's — must not re-run it.

## `story.ni:5925-5934`. DAVID: "Jacqueline" and "respectable young lady" are
## Theresa's words for Jack — under §4/§3g they convert to the boy words;
## carried as the source has them, yours to change.

define phrase curfew-arrival
  You slip quietly through the front door, into the familiar smells of cooking
  grease and laundry soap; the familiar sight of shabby, warped floorboards lit
  by dim, flickering tallow lamps. It's nothing fancy, but it's home, and
  you're familiar enough with the plight of most of Toresal's orphans to know
  that you're lucky to have it.
end phrase

define phrase curfew-theresa
  Widow Theresa, who probably has been lurking here all day, just waiting for
  you to return, springs out from her corner and slams the front door shut.
  'The evening churchbells are ringing, Jacqueline,' she tells you, her mouth
  set in a satisfied little line. 'Well past the time when any respectable
  young lady should be out on the streets.' She locks the door with a long
  brass key that she wears on a ribbon around her neck.
end phrase

define phrase theresa-th1-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — conversation TH1: "You're filthy," Theresa
  begins — the hallway tree, TH1-11; Theresa does NOT see through the
  disguise, and dislikes her.)
end phrase

## `story.ni:5826` — after the waking, the hallway is clear.

define phrase tiptoe
  You tiptoe out into the hallway. Widow Theresa is nowhere in sight.
end phrase

## `story.ni:6263`, `stopping`.

define phrase theresa-blocks-privy, stopping
  'Not so fast,' snaps Widow Theresa, blocking your path. 'Don't think I don't
  know what you're up to.'

  'But I really have to go to the bathroom!' you protest, knowing that it
  would do no good even if it were true.

  Theresa smirks. 'Then you can do it with the door open, while I stand and
  watch. No? Well then, I guess you can hold it until the morning. Until then,
  you're not going anywhere but to bed.'
or
  It's no use; Widow Theresa is watching you like a hawk. You'll probably have
  to wait until everyone goes to sleep before making your escape.
end phrase

## The inside of the door (`story.ni:5790`), and its refusals.

define phrase door-locked-curfew
  The front door is closed and locked — and it will stay locked until sunup.
  The Widows give you a good bit of latitude generally, but curfew is one of
  the few rules they never, ever bend.
end phrase

