define phrase not-tired
  You're not even close to being tired. You couldn't possibly go to sleep
  until you've seen what Bobby wanted to show you.
end phrase

## `story.ni:5985`, up to where the new scene takes over ("You snap awake"
## is replaced by the knock).

define phrase bunk-dozing
  Grumbling, you climb into your bunk and make yourself as comfortable as you
  can on the lumpy mattress. The other children eventually lie down and fall
  quiet, and Widow Fiona stops in to blow out the light. Soon all you can hear
  are the soft snores of children and the occasional creaking of a floorboard.
  You are determined to keep your eyes open until you are sure Theresa has
  left the hallway, but soon your muscles, tense from the day's chase, are
  unknotting... your breath is coming slower, heavier...
end phrase

define phrase door-knock-stub
  (TODO during play-testing — THE NEW SCENE: a knock wakes Jack. From her
  bunk, with the children asleep around her, she hears Widow Theresa hold the
  front door closed against a mercenary; he leaves — "I'll be back!" — and
  Jack quietly waits for Theresa's footsteps to return to her room before she
  slips out of bed. The knock, both voices, the parting word, and the wait
  are yours; the wait is narrated here, not spent.)
end phrase

## The children see her (§3c, the talent-in-children rule); nothing in the
## source's text for them outs her, and nothing here does.

create the orphans
  aka orphan, children, child, kids, kid, urchins, urchin, boys, girls, boy, girl, other orphans, younger children
  a person, plural
  in the Dormitory
  states, reversible: awake, asleep

  The kids are mostly younger than you. Some of them you saw earlier with
  Bobby. They sit up in their beds, whispering secrets to each other, showing
  off the childish treasures they found during the day: pebbles, pennies, bits
  of string. It almost makes you wish you could be that young again. Your life
  was so much less complicated then.

  phrase detail while the orphans is asleep:
    The other children are all sound asleep in their bunks.

  on the player talking
    refuse when the orphans is asleep: dont-wake-them
    refuse not-in-the-mood
  end on

define phrase not-in-the-mood
  You're not really in the mood for a chat. As you've gotten older, and the
  other kids your age have drifted away from the orphanage, you find you have
  little in common with the younger children.
end phrase

define phrase dont-wake-them
  You don't want to wake them. They'll just cause a commotion, and then you'll
  never be able to sneak out.
end phrase

