  end on

## The window: stuck halfway, the way out. `climbed` is the chapter's end and
## Chapter IV's trigger. `climbable, enterable` are the mechanical traits the
## center post uses (`market-scenery.chord`): they let the standard action
## validate so the clause's `move` can ride through. Going through it moves Jack to the spur behind the
## house with Gentry's escape text (`story.ni:6338`, the off-the-bench
## variant).

create the privy window
  aka window, narrow window, stuck window, pane, sill, glass, dirty glass
  scenery, climbable, enterable
  in the Privy
  states: shut-in, climbed

  The window has been stuck halfway open for as long as you can remember. No
  one's ever bothered to fix it, because no matter how cold it gets, no one
  who's been in that privy would ever consider closing the window for even an
  instant. There's a bit less than two hand-widths of clearance between the
  pane and the sill — just enough for you to scrape through.

  on the player opening
    refuse window-stuck
  end on

  on the player closing
    refuse window-stuck
  end on

  on the player searching
    refuse window-search-inside
  end on

  on the player entering
    phrase window-escape when before gallows
    phrase shannon-through-the-window when the raid is keyed
    move Widow Shannon to the Behind Maiden House when the raid is keyed
    change the company to escorting when the raid is keyed
    change the privy window to climbed
    change the raid to escaped when the raid is keyed
    move the player to the Behind Maiden House
  end on

  on the player climbing
    phrase window-escape when before gallows
    phrase shannon-through-the-window when the raid is keyed
    move Widow Shannon to the Behind Maiden House when the raid is keyed
    change the company to escorting when the raid is keyed
    change the privy window to climbed
    change the raid to escaped when the raid is keyed
    move the player to the Behind Maiden House
  end on

## The second climb, after the raid (`story.ni:10010-10025`), is Chapter 8's
## opening beat — Shannon comes out after her (`red-gate.chord` carries the
## text) — and Chapter VIII's row begins on `escaped`.

define phrase hole-too-small
  It's way too small for you to fit into. Thank the Goddesses.
end phrase

define phrase hole-less-said
  The less said about that, the better.
end phrase

define phrase bench-wobbles
  You get onto the bench. It wobbles under your feet, and you grab the window
  sill to steady yourself.
end phrase

define phrase window-stuck
  The window is adamantly stuck halfway; the pane won't budge an inch, up or
  down.
end phrase

## Carried as the source has it (`story.ni:6322`): its `[if location is the
## Privy]` branch says "the privy room inside Maiden House" from INSIDE.

define phrase window-search-inside
  Through the window's grime-smeared glass, you can see the privy room inside
  Maiden House.
end phrase

define phrase window-escape
  You sneak a furtive glance back at the hallway, then step up onto the bench.
  It wobbles under your feet, and you grab the window sill to steady yourself.

  Then, holding your breath to keep from making a sound, you pull yourself up
  and through. The window is at ground level outside, and you wriggle out onto
  the slick cobblestones.

  You're free! Of course, the widows will find out you're gone soon enough —
  Maiden House is not a very big place, after all. But you've snuck out
  before, and they know you can take care of yourself. At worst, you'll pull
  extra laundry duty for a few days.
end phrase

